openstack: select the catalog interface with OS_INTERFACE

ServiceClientFromProvider in internal/openstack/service_client.go
builds every OpenStack client of the operator (compute, placement,
image and network, through GetServiceClient) from an empty
gophercloud.EndpointOpts, and ApplyDefaults sets its Availability to
public. hvo therefore reads the catalog's public endpoints alone. In a
cluster whose public URLs do not resolve from a pod, such as a gateway
name that resolves to the loopback address, the operator reaches no
service, while the catalog's internal endpoints, the in-cluster
Service URLs, would serve it.

OS_INTERFACE, the variable the OpenStack clients read for the same
choice, now selects the interface: public, internal or admin. Unset or
empty keeps public, upstream's behaviour. The value is matched as
given, with no trimming and no case folding. Any other value is an
error before the endpoint locator runs, so every controller's
SetupWithManager returns it and the operator exits at start instead of
falling back to public unnoticed. An error of the locator,
ErrEndpointNotFound when the catalog has no endpoint of that interface,
is returned unchanged.

No upstream test covers the endpoint selection. The patch adds
TestServiceClientInterface, a plain Go test outside the Ginkgo suite
with an endpoint locator that records the options it gets, so it runs
without envtest; the CobaltCore image build runs it and fails when it
does not pass.

Applies-to: cobaltcore-dev/openstack-hypervisor-operator main @ a2baf3f4002e39ff93f105fae9594b357ba55b21

Upstream status: not submitted

